# Component design

## Components render, nothing else

A component is a **view** — the humble, outermost part of the driving side: it
receives data and callbacks and renders them. Behaviour lives one step in — in
the presentation model (`{{PRESENTATION_SKILL}}`), and rules live in the domain.

- **No state, effect or memoisation in a view** ({{VIEW_STATE_HOOKS}}), and no
  derived state. If you want one, that logic belongs in the presentation model:
  build the rest and report `STATUS: blocked` naming the exact keys it must
  expose (`isDialogOpen`, `openDialog`, `canSubmit`).
- **The one narrow exception:** {{VIEW_REF_EXCEPTION}}. Purely ephemeral
  presentation state with no logic and no link to any presentation-model value
  (a hover flag, a tooltip open) is also acceptable. The lowest tier wrapping a
  platform primitive's own interaction (focus, pressed state, a controlled
  input) is the same exception.
- **No application access.** A view never reaches the use cases, the
  composition root's context or a store. Its presentation model does.
- **No effect that triggers work.** Loading on mount, refreshing on a route
  change, analytics, navigation: the presentation model does it.
- **No business constants or status checks.** Render the boolean the
  presentation model exposes (`canSubmit`), never a status compared inline or
  a cap checked in markup.
- **Handlers only forward.** An event handler calls one presentation-model
  callback; it does not guard, sequence or clear fields.
- **Every visible string is translated** (`{{I18N_SKILL}}`).

State gets smuggled into views under innocent names. Each of these belongs in
the presentation model:

| Smuggled in as… | Actually is |
|---|---|
| "just a controlled input" — local state for a draft, query or filter | a value and setter the presentation model owns |
| "just presentation" — `canSubmit` computed from a value and a loading flag | derived display state |
| "just wiring" — a handler that guards, calls the model, then clears a field | orchestration |
| "just a default" — local state seeded from a prop or model value | an initial value the model owns |

## Tiers

{{COMPONENT_TIERS_TABLE}}

A lower tier never imports a higher one. A reusable tier never imports a
feature folder.

## Reuse first

Before creating a component, search, in order: the lowest tier (is there a
button, input, icon that fits?), then the reusable composites, then the
feature's siblings. Create only what is genuinely new. Extend an existing
component with a variant rather than forking a near-copy.

## Size and splitting

A component does one nameable thing. Split it when any of these is true:

- its markup runs past roughly 80–100 lines (imports and types excluded);
- it renders two or more sections you could describe independently;
- a conditional branch is large enough to stand alone;
- it renders non-trivial markup per item of a collection — extract the item;
- you are about to write a comment like "left column" or "participants
  section" — that comment marks a component boundary.

Test for a split: if you deleted the block, would the parent still make sense?
Then extract it, passing only the props it needs — never a whole object "in
case". Name it in plain words (`ParticipantCard`, `ReportListItem`); if no
good name comes, the boundary is wrong.

Do not over-split: a two-line fragment used once, with no identity of its own,
stays inline. This is `extract-til-you-drop` applied to markup.

## Props

- Props are typed explicitly, in the project's own vocabulary.
- Data in, callbacks out. A component never receives a use case, a port or a
  store.
- Display-ready values come in already derived; the component does not compute
  them from raw domain data.

